# Chapter 01 GitHub fragment collision

Read-only diagnosis of root's saved actual Chromium DOM at fixed commit 64deb4b080afba1872b4c33d371eeae8bc3f29c2. Source/alias Git blobs were independently obtained; embedded rawLines reconstruct the fixed generated Markdown exactly. No source, aliases, renderer or script used by root was edited. This is not a new independent browser run.

The two matches are not two distinct headings. An empty explicit alias A with name=user-content-3-surface-descriptorとbootstrap is in a paragraph immediately before the markdown-heading DIV. That DIV contains the single H2 3. surface descriptorとbootstrap, followed by GitHub's own permalink A with id=user-content-3-surface-descriptorとbootstrap. Thus these are two DOM targets at neighboring positions belonging to the same logical section. They are not identical DOM positions; pixel separation was not measured. Technically this is a name/id fragment collision, not two duplicate id attributes. The selector intentionally covering both correctly rejects it. The stable n-626f6f747374726170 target occurs once. No other id/name collision is present in the saved article elements.

The bounded correction is to remove just bootstrap's redundant explicit legacy alias and preserve its actual GitHub automatic heading ID, as was done for two chapter 13 headings. Preserve the typed Section bootstrap and its n- anchor. Do not change source wording to provoke a different slug, rename the old fragment, select the first match silently, or count two targets as an unconditional pass. The fixed result must again be rendered on GitHub to verify the legacy target and its heading ownership. This recommendation is platform-specific to verified GitHub behavior and does not claim all Markdown renderers generate that ID.

Root's test currently derives both old fragments and stable Section IDs from the remaining alias JSON. Removing bootstrap's alias would silently remove BOTH its legacy and n- fragment from testing. Keep an independent fixed list of all eight old fragments (the original preflight mapping) and all seven actual Section IDs. Then navigation coverage remains 15 rather than dropping to 13. Retain rawLines identity checking. Root's current record contains four successful legacy navigation rows; bootstrap and subsequent cases were not executed successfully, and this failure does not invalidate those four earlier observations.

The original preflight already reported this possible collision because bootstrap's title contains no Ruby. Actual saved DOM now confirms it. The old snapshot's original 8 headings and the new generated title have corresponding meaning; this diagnostic does not reopen or certify the author's complete prose review.

check.py passes exact Git/source rawLines, actual adjacent DOM match, one collision only, and unique n-bootstrap checks. Independent focus/navigation, fixed re-render and final migration acceptance remain unexecuted.

## Follow-up: retain the legacy alias in both outputs

Root requires the old fragment in generated HTML as well as GitHub Markdown. The initial alias-removal recommendation above considered the observed GitHub permalink only and is therefore insufficient for that two-output condition; it is superseded, not applied. The failure snapshot remains unchanged.

Root's proposed heading is `3. surface descriptorとGrammarのbootstrap`, retaining the old explicit alias and the stable Section ID in both outputs. The old paragraph first describes all language surface definitions and Grammar compiler package generation; the next explicitly says Grammar自身の初期package, 同一のGrammar syntax, no smaller Grammar dialect, and using the seed to read its own source. Identifying the bootstrap as Grammar's therefore makes the existing referent explicit without narrowing the preceding all-language surface contract or changing any seed/build failure obligation. This is a review of that one proposed heading only, not a new full chapter review. No source text has been edited by the reviewer. The new actual GitHub automatic ID and uniqueness/navigation still require regeneration and verification; they are not inferred as passed from this semantic assessment.

## Corrected output scope after direct canonical::html inspection

The preceding follow-up's premise that both outputs carry legacy aliases was incorrect. At fixed 9c5883b, tools/src/doc/canonical.rs::html (lines 223-248) passes only page id, logical projection path, physical source path, HTML route and source bytes to export::pages::generate. It does not read/pass alias JSON to that backend. Therefore legacy aliases are part of the GitHub Markdown projection only; HTML retains the seven semantic n-Section IDs. No previous paragraph should be used as evidence that HTML had legacy aliases or that alias removal would remove an existing HTML legacy target. Root explicitly corrected this premise, and the reviewer verified it directly. The one-heading semantic assessment remains valid, and the original collision/failure bytes remain unchanged. New cutover checks under ../review-canonical01-cutover test eight legacy plus seven semantic targets on GitHub, and only seven semantic targets plus the real chapter 22 link in HTML.
